      ":question/id": "d87dffbe-9b03-5e1f-b22d-9dc0376574f7",
      ":question/problem": "Let $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}e^x$ and $g(x)=8x-\\frac{2}{3}x^3$.\n\n$\\int (4f(x)+3g(x))\\,\\text{d}x\\,=${{field-1}}\n$+C,\\,$ where $C$ is an arbitrary constant.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We combine the sum and the constant multiple rules, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4f(x)+3g(x))\\text{d}x & =\\int (4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}+3(8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}))\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2{e}^{x}+24x-2{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+24\\int x\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}+24\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}+12{x}^{2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant."
    },
